1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,864
Every service operation runs on a stream of updates: notes, calls, messages, systems.

2
00:00:07,044 --> 00:00:12,650
The hard part is moving that information accurately, and finding it again when it matters.

3
00:00:13,030 --> 00:00:19,093
When that breaks down, time disappears: people ask again, search, and re-type.

4
00:00:19,273 --> 00:00:24,883
Resources get over-committed: a job is double-booked, or a part is ordered twice.

5
00:00:25,063 --> 00:00:31,276
None of it is inevitable. Capture updates easily, from any operation, in one place.

6
00:00:31,456 --> 00:00:37,616
Then supervisors can look up the state of operations, ask a question, and get a straight answer.

7
00:00:38,296 --> 00:00:43,864
That is our solution: the Service Operations Agent, powered by Deloitte and NVIDIA.

8
00:00:44,544 --> 00:00:48,718
Built for any service operation, and shown today in automotive service.

9
00:00:49,398 --> 00:00:53,618
Here is how it works. An update goes in once, spoken or typed.

10
00:00:53,798 --> 00:00:57,831
Riva turns speech into text, and Nemotron extracts the facts.

11
00:00:58,011 --> 00:01:01,808
Code checks every fact against the record, and keeps an audit trail.

12
00:01:01,988 --> 00:01:05,513
Supervisors then simply ask, and get cited answers.

13
00:01:06,343 --> 00:01:10,327
Let's follow one update, live. A technician dictates it.

14
00:01:10,507 --> 00:01:12,871
NVIDIA Riva transcribes the speech.

15
00:01:13,051 --> 00:01:17,041
Nemotron extracts the facts: the measurement and the spec.

16
00:01:17,371 --> 00:01:21,684
Code verifies it and flags the repair order unsafe, immediately.

17
00:01:22,514 --> 00:01:24,969
Now everyone sees the same live picture.

18
00:01:25,149 --> 00:01:27,785
Every job, with the reason it needs attention.

19
00:01:27,965 --> 00:01:32,327
18 vehicles flagged unsafe, including the one just logged.

20
00:01:33,157 --> 00:01:37,984
A supervisor asks: which vehicles cannot be released on safety grounds?

21
00:01:38,164 --> 00:01:42,154
18 vehicles, each one cited, in a tenth of a second.

22
00:01:42,484 --> 00:01:46,430
Another question: which parts hold up more than one job?

23
00:01:46,610 --> 00:01:49,534
Order each part once, and clear several jobs.

24
00:01:49,864 --> 00:01:54,220
An open question: has anyone seen a whistling noise on a Passat?

25
00:01:54,400 --> 00:01:59,217
Search, reranking and a cited answer, in seconds, on one GPU.

26
00:02:00,047 --> 00:02:04,828
Behind it all: three NVIDIA NIMs on a single L40S.

27
00:02:05,208 --> 00:02:09,478
Riva handles speech, and NeMo Guardrails stops unsafe requests.

28
00:02:09,658 --> 00:02:12,069
NeMo Evaluator scores every release.

29
00:02:12,899 --> 00:02:15,590
All of it observable, live in Grafana.

30
00:02:16,420 --> 00:02:21,005
In short: updates go in once, everyone sees one state.

31
00:02:21,185 --> 00:02:24,479
Every answer in a tenth of a second, traced to a record.

32
00:02:24,659 --> 00:02:27,161
Fast. Grounded. Yours to run.

